@meta
id: 16-08
title: Done with it
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: buffer-list, buffer-switch
par: 145
minutes: 6
vscode: the x on the tab, or middle-click
open: order.entity.ts order.repository.ts errors.json

@brief
A buffer stays in the list until you remove it. After a morning of
`:b` the list is long, the fragments stop being unique, and Tab shows
you six candidates. `:bd` deletes the current buffer from the list.
The file on disk is untouched; vim just stops holding it. The window
that was showing it moves on to another buffer, and `:ls` gets one
line shorter.

`:bd` will not delete a buffer with unsaved changes; it says E89 and
waits. Write first, or `:bd!` to discard. It also takes a name or a
number, so you can close a buffer you are not looking at.

The ticket is a refund timestamp, and the working style is: finish a
file, close its buffer, move on.

  1. `order.entity.ts`: `refundedAt?: Date;` directly under
     `cancellationReason`.
  2. `order.repository.ts`: `refundedAt: record.refundedAt ?? undefined,`
     directly under the `cancellationReason` line in `toEntity`.
  3. `errors.json`: nothing. It is the last buffer standing when the
     other two are gone, and `:q` from there ends the session.

@keys
| `:bd` | delete the current buffer from the list |
| `:bd 2` `:bd name` | delete another one by number or fragment |
| `:bd!` | delete it and discard its unsaved changes |
| `:ls` | confirm it is gone |

@hints
Entity: `/cancellationReason`, `o`, the field, `Esc`, `:w`, then `:bd`.
You land in the repository because it is next in the list.
--
Same shape in the repository. `:w` before `:bd`; try `:bd` first
without writing if you want to see E89.
--
`:ls` after the second `:bd` shows only `errors.json`. `:q`.

@solution
    /cancellationReason<Enter>  o refundedAt?: Date; <Esc>  :w<Enter>  :bd<Enter>
    /cancellationReason<Enter>  o refundedAt: record.refundedAt ?? undefined, <Esc>  :w<Enter>  :bd<Enter>
    :q<Enter>

About a hundred and twenty-five keystrokes; the typed code is most of
them and the two `:bd` are eight.

The instinct from VS Code is that closing is tidying, and there is some
truth in it: a short list is one where every fragment is unique. But do
not over-tidy. A buffer costs nothing to keep, `Ctrl-^` and `:b` only
work on buffers that are still in the list, and reopening a file you
closed two minutes ago is the buffer equivalent of re-finding a tab.
The habit worth having is to close a buffer when the task that needed
it is finished, not every time you look away from it.

`:bd` on the last buffer leaves you with an empty one rather than
quitting; `:q` is still how you leave.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
  refundedAt?: Date;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
      refundedAt: record.refundedAt ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
